## Default VM args shipped inside the release tarball.
## OTP requires a node name, but the default release keeps distribution
## entirely local: the distribution listener and epmd are explicitly disabled,
## and no cookie is shipped. An operator who needs distribution access supplies
## their own -sname and a random -setcookie, and re-enables the listener with
## -dist_listen true.
##
## -dist_listen false is deliberate, and is not the same as leaving it out:
## erl(1) says "by default a node will listen for incoming distribution
## connections", so an absent flag would make the default install reachable. It
## also implies -hidden. See #W1NX4KP for why the release check asserts this
## posture rather than any particular way of achieving it, and why the choice of
## per-install distribution policy belongs to the deployment repository.
## Kernel poll keeps socket wakeups off the polling loop (an I2P router is
## mostly sockets).
-sname i2per
-dist_listen false
-start_epmd false
+K true

## Scheduler busy-wait thresholds, set on purpose (#VXRH456). The emulator
## default is `medium` for normal schedulers and `short` for dirty CPU
## schedulers (erts_early_init_scheduling, unchanged OTP 25 -> OTP 29), so the
## tree already runs warm and this is a cost bound, not a fix: measured
## against the soak's own transit load, ingress latency into i2p_tunnel_srv
## is flat at p50 1-2 us quiet, 37 us at 50 frames/s and 152 us saturated,
## the same for none, very_short, medium, long and very_long at +S 1, +S 2
## and +S 16. What the threshold does move is idle CPU on a many-core host:
## at a 100 Hz wake pattern the default `medium` costs up to ~12% of one
## core and `very_long` ~43%, while `very_short` costs ~6%. This release
## therefore ships the shortest threshold with a spin at all -- the value
## with no measured latency change and the smallest burn.
+sbwt very_short
+sbwtdcpu very_short
